.Dd September 26, 2026
.Dt TUNEFS_MINIXFS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tunefs_minixfs
.Nd change the settings of a MINIX file system
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl fN
.Op Fl B Cm le | be
.Op Fl c Cm clean | dirty
.Op Fl e Cm 0 | 1
.Op Fl l Cm 14 | 30
.Op Fl m Cm minix | linux | Ar bytes
.Op Fl M Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
.Op Fl s Ar blocks Op Fl k
.Ar image
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
changes the settings of the MINIX file system in
.Ar image ;
see
.Xr minixfs 5 .
Without options it prints them: the byte order, the name length, the
size in blocks, whether the file system is marked clean, the maximum
file size with the one MINIX works out, and the bits of the maps past
the last inode or zone.
Each change is printed as the old and the new value.
The byte order is changed first, then the name length, then the size.
The image is locked with
.Xr fcntl 2
while it is written, and one that another program holds is refused.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl N
Print what the other options would change, and write nothing.
.It Fl B Cm le | be
Store every number of the file system in the other byte order,
little-endian as on the PC or big-endian as on the Atari ST and the
Amiga: the super block, the words of the bit maps, the inodes, the
indirect zones and the inode numbers of directory entries.
Everything is read before anything is written.
.It Fl l Cm 14 | 30
Give a V1 or V2 file system names of 14 characters, as in MINIX, or 30,
the extension of Linux: the magic number changes, and every directory is
written anew with entries of the new size.
Every directory is read, every name checked and the zones counted before
anything is written; names too long for 14 characters are all listed,
and then nothing changes, as when the larger directories would not fit.
.It Fl s Ar blocks
Grow or shrink the file system, and the image file with it unless
.Fl k
is given or the image is a device, to
.Ar blocks
blocks, cut down to whole zones.
Used zones are relocated as needed, and growing the zone map may
require additional space for relocating metadata and data.
If there is insufficient space, resizing makes no changes.
Earlier changes may remain when combined with
.Fl l ;
see
.Sx CAVEATS .
Shrinking keeps the zone map's block count, which
.Nm fsck_minixfs Fl w
may report as excessive.
If the image size is retained, it must already hold the requested size.
A device whose size cannot be determined cannot be grown.
.It Fl k
With
.Fl s ,
keep the size of the image file: the file system shrinks inside it and
leaves what is past its new end as it was, or grows as far as the file
holds.
.It Fl f
Change a file system that is not marked clean with
.Fl B ,
.Fl l
and
.Fl s
all the same; see
.Sx CAVEATS .
.It Fl c Cm clean | dirty
Mark the file system clean or dirty: in V1 and V2 in the state field
that Linux keeps, in V3 in the flags of MINIX 3, which mounts a file
system that is not clean read-only, and in Minix-vmd in its flags.
.It Fl e Cm 0 | 1
Set the bits of each map past the last inode or zone to 0, as the mkfs
of MINIX leaves them, or 1, as that of Linux sets them.
.It Fl m Cm minix | linux | Ar bytes
Set the maximum file size in the super block to what MINIX works out
from the zones that the double indirect zone reaches, as
.Xr newfs_minixfs 8
writes it by default, to what mkfs.minix of Linux writes, or to
.Ar bytes ,
from 1 to 2147483647.
Linux and the tools of this package keep files to it; see CAVEATS in
.Xr minixfs 5 .
A size below that of the largest file is refused, and nothing changes.
.It Fl M Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
Modify an image containing the file system on one disk side.
See
.Xr minixfs 1
for the track layout.
Its size cannot change.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with 0 on success, 1 if anything failed, and 2 for a usage error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Turn a disk of the Atari ST into one for the PC, with the names of
Linux:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ tunefs_minixfs -B le -l 30 atari.img
.Ed
.Pp
Double the size of an image:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ tunefs_minixfs -s 2880 floppy.img
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr minixfs 1 ,
.Xr minixfs 5 ,
.Xr fsck_minixfs 8 ,
.Xr newfs_minixfs 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Shoichi Fukusaka
.Sh CAVEATS
Before using
.Fl B ,
.Fl l
or
.Fl s ,
unmount the image, check it with
.Xr fsck_minixfs 8
and keep a backup.
Interruption can leave the conversion incomplete.
These options require a clean image unless
.Fl f
is given; forcing a change does not make it safe on a mounted file system.
.Nm fsck_minixfs Fl y
marks a consistent image clean, including V1 and V2 images created by
MINIX without a clean flag.
.Pp
Invalid requests are rejected before writing.
When combining
.Fl l
and
.Fl s ,
resizing may fail because the converted directories change the space
required.
The error reports any changes already applied.
.Pp
.Fl B
and
.Fl l
do not apply to Minix-vmd.
